{-
   A declaration with no body, and nothing saying who supplies one.

   The parser accepts the shape everywhere now. It used to refuse it unless the file came from
   `lib/Core` or `lib/Native` - which made "may this declaration have no body" a question about which
   directory a file is in, decided in the parser, which cannot see the one thing that answers it.
   `@builtin(role)` on the declaration says the compiler supplies the body, and that is a fact about
   the declaration rather than about its location.

   So the refusal moved here, where the role is visible, and the message names what is missing rather
   than the token the parser wanted next. It is also the shape a foreign declaration and a
   shared-library interface will want: a signature with no body and an attribute saying where the
   body is.
-}

fn orphan(x: I32) -> I32

fn main() -> I32 = 0
